Chapter: GopUsbMouse
  cites Works chapter GopXhci
  cites Works chapter GopAhci
  cites Works chapter GopUsbKbd
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe

 USB HID boot mouse over GopXhci: the pointer half of H2. A modern
 machine has no PS/2 mouse any more than it has a PS/2 keyboard; the
 pointer arrives as 3-byte boot reports on an interrupt endpoint --
 byte zero the buttons (bit 0 left, bit 1 right, bit 2 middle), bytes
 one and two signed 8-bit deltas. This chapter fetches those reports
 with the same three-phase pump the keyboard uses and folds them into
 an absolute position clamped to the panel, because every consumer of
 a pointer wants "where is it now", not "how did it just twitch".

 The boot-protocol interface is class 3, subclass 1, protocol 2 --
 the request path (SET_PROTOCOL 0) and the endpoint bring-up are the
 keyboard's, reused. A device that only speaks its report-descriptor
 protocol (an absolute tablet, say) is not matched: this driver
 decodes exactly the boot report and nothing else.

 The pump's idle path allocates nothing -- the same law the keyboard
 pump earned the hard way: it runs from a poll loop on a machine with
 no garbage collector, and a record per call is a halt in seconds.
 One report is consumed whole in one call (a delta has no
 convergence walk), and the click is edge-detected at consume time so
 a press-and-release between two polls still registers once.

 We say:

Section: Mouse State

 The state block carries the phase and the folded pointer: [0] phase,
 [4] x, [8] y, [12] buttons, [16] click edge (left button went down in
 the last consumed report), [20] event count (probe visibility).

  um-ph-idle : Integer = 0
  um-ph-armed : Integer = 1

 A machine can carry more than one boot-mouse interface, and the one a
 person moves is not obliged to be the first the walk meets -- the same
 defect the keyboard had, cured the same day it was proven on the ASUS
 (four keyboard-shaped interfaces, keys on the fourth). Peers each pump
 their own endpoint but fold into ONE position block (`um-pos`), because
 the desktop has one cursor: phase and the per-interface event count
 stay in the interface's own `um-state`, position, buttons and the
 click edge live at `um-pos`, which for the primary is its own state
 block and for every peer is the primary's.

  UsbMouse = record {
    um-ok : Boolean,
    um-host : XhciHost,
    um-dev : UsbDev,
    um-dci : Integer,
    um-ring : Integer,
    um-report : Integer,
    um-state : Integer,
    um-pos : Integer,
    um-peers : List UsbMouse
  }

  mouse-no-mouse : UsbMouse
  mouse-no-mouse = UsbMouse { um-ok = False, um-host = xhci-no-host, um-dev = xhci-no-dev, um-dci = 0, um-ring = 0, um-report = 0, um-state = 0, um-pos = 0, um-peers = [] }

  mouse-peer-cap : Integer = 3

  mouse-peers : UsbMouse -> Integer
  mouse-peers (m) = if m.um-ok == False then 0 else list-length (m.um-peers)

  mouse-peer : UsbMouse, Integer -> UsbMouse
  mouse-peer (m) (i) = list-at (m.um-peers) i

  mouse-adopt : UsbMouse, UsbMouse -> UsbMouse
  mouse-adopt (m) (p) =
    if list-length (m.um-peers) >= mouse-peer-cap then m
    else let w = list-push (m.um-peers) p
    in __record-set m "um-peers" w

  mouse-x : UsbMouse -> Integer
  mouse-x (m) = peek-32 (m.um-pos) 4

  mouse-y : UsbMouse -> Integer
  mouse-y (m) = peek-32 (m.um-pos) 8

  mouse-buttons : UsbMouse -> Integer
  mouse-buttons (m) = peek-32 (m.um-pos) 12

  mouse-clicked : UsbMouse -> Boolean
  mouse-clicked (m) = peek-32 (m.um-pos) 16 /= 0

  mouse-events : UsbMouse -> Integer
  mouse-events (m) = if m.um-ok == False then 0 else peek-32 (m.um-state) 20

  mouse-warp : UsbMouse, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  mouse-warp (m) (x) (y) =
    if m.um-ok == False then 0
    else let sx = poke-32 (m.um-pos) 4 x
    in let sy = poke-32 (m.um-pos) 8 y
    in um-publish x y (peek-32 (m.um-pos) 12)

Section: Pointer Mailbox

 The foreword's InputSource cannot cite a Works driver, so the folded
 pointer is published to fixed cells and the foreword reads the
 cells: 36736 carries [0] magic "PTR1", [4] x, [8] y, [12] buttons.
 The magic is written LAST, at open, after the fields are zeroed --
 the same guard the disk handles use, so firmware residue cannot
 impersonate a pointer. Consume and warp keep the cells current;
 the idle pump path never touches them.

  um-cells : Integer = 36736
  um-cells-magic : Integer = #31525450

  um-publish : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  um-publish (x) (y) (buttons) =
    let px = poke-32 um-cells 4 x
    in let py = poke-32 um-cells 8 y
    in poke-32 um-cells 12 buttons

  um-publish-magic : Integer -> Integer
  um-publish-magic (dummy) =
    let z = um-publish 0 0 0
    in poke-32 um-cells 0 um-cells-magic

Section: Interface

 The keyboard's descriptor walk, retargeted at protocol 2.

  mouse-find-interface : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  mouse-find-interface (cfg) (off) (total) =
    if off >= total then -1
    else let len = peek-byte cfg off
    in if len == 0 then -1
    else if peek-byte cfg (off + 1) /= 4 then mouse-find-interface cfg (off + len) total
    else if peek-byte cfg (off + 5) /= 3 then mouse-find-interface cfg (off + len) total
    else if peek-byte cfg (off + 6) /= 1 then mouse-find-interface cfg (off + len) total
    else if peek-byte cfg (off + 7) /= 2 then mouse-find-interface cfg (off + len) total
    else off

Section: Pure Decode

 The deltas are signed 8-bit two's complement, and the folded
 position is clamped to the panel so the pointer parks at an edge
 instead of leaving the screen.

  mouse-sext : Integer -> Integer
  mouse-sext (b) = if b > 127 then b - 256 else b

  mouse-clamp : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  mouse-clamp (v) (limit) =
    if v < 0 then 0
    else if v >= limit then limit - 1
    else v

  mouse-fold : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  mouse-fold (pos) (delta-byte) (limit) =
    mouse-clamp (pos + mouse-sext delta-byte) limit

  mouse-click-edge : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  mouse-click-edge (prev-buttons) (buttons) =
    if bit-and prev-buttons 1 == 0 then bit-and buttons 1 else 0

Section: Open

 The tail of the keyboard's endpoint bring-up, minted as a mouse. The
 pointer starts at the origin; the screen that adopts it warps it to
 wherever it wants the cursor born.

  mouse-open-endpoint : XhciHost, UsbDev, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> UsbMouse
  mouse-open-endpoint (xh) (dev) (iface) (cfgv) (epnum) (maxpkt) (binterval) =
    mouse-open-iface xh dev iface cfgv epnum maxpkt binterval True 0

 The peer form skips SET_CONFIGURATION when the device is already
 configured (USB 2.0 9.1.1.5: re-sending it resets every data toggle
 the device holds) and folds into the position block it is handed;
 zero means own the position, the primary's case.

  mouse-open-iface : XhciHost, UsbDev, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Boolean, Integer -> UsbMouse
  mouse-open-iface (xh) (dev) (iface) (cfgv) (epnum) (maxpkt) (binterval) (do-cfg) (pos) =
    let sc = if do-cfg then usb-set-configuration xh dev cfgv else True
    in if sc == False then mouse-no-mouse
    else let bp = usb-set-protocol xh dev iface 0
    in if bp == False then mouse-no-mouse
    else let dci = epnum * 2 + 1
    in let ring = xhci-ring-new
    in let r0 = xhci-set-ep-ring (dev.ud-ctx) dci (xhci-ring-trbs ring) (xh.xh-ctxsz)
    in let ictx = xhci-ictx-single dci 7 maxpkt (xhci-ring-trbs ring) (xhci-ep-interval (dev.ud-speed) binterval) (xh.xh-ctxsz) (dev.ud-ctx)
    in let ce = xhci-configure-endpoint xh dev ictx
    in if ce.xe-ok == False then mouse-no-mouse
    else if ce.xe-code /= trb-cc-success then mouse-no-mouse
    else let pub = if pos == 0 then um-publish-magic 0 else 0
    in let st = alloc-zeroed 64 64
    in UsbMouse { um-ok = True, um-host = xh, um-dev = dev, um-dci = dci, um-ring = ring, um-report = alloc-zeroed 64 64, um-state = st, um-pos = if pos == 0 then st else pos, um-peers = [] }

Section: The Pump

 Arm one interrupt IN transfer; check the event ring without
 blocking through the per-endpoint latch (another wait on the shared
 host may already have consumed this endpoint's completion -- on a
 combo receiver the keyboard pump polls this very slot); consume the
 report whole. Returns 1 exactly when a report was folded, so the
 caller knows to move the cursor.

  mouse-set-phase : UsbMouse, Integer -> Integer
  mouse-set-phase (m) (ph) =
    let w = poke-32 (m.um-state) 0 ph
    in 0

  mouse-arm : UsbMouse -> Integer
  mouse-arm (m) =
    let w = xhci-ring-push (m.um-ring) (m.um-report) 0 8 (bit-or (bit-shl trb-t-normal 10) trb-flag-ioc)
    in let db = xhci-ring-doorbell (m.um-host) ((m.um-dev).ud-slot) (m.um-dci)
    in mouse-set-phase m um-ph-armed

  mouse-consume : UsbMouse, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  mouse-consume (m) (w) (h) =
    let buttons = peek-byte (m.um-report) 0
    in let prev = peek-32 (m.um-pos) 12
    in let nx = mouse-fold (peek-32 (m.um-pos) 4) (peek-byte (m.um-report) 1) w
    in let ny = mouse-fold (peek-32 (m.um-pos) 8) (peek-byte (m.um-report) 2) h
    in let s0 = poke-32 (m.um-pos) 4 nx
    in let s1 = poke-32 (m.um-pos) 8 ny
    in let s2 = poke-32 (m.um-pos) 12 buttons
    in let s3 = poke-32 (m.um-pos) 16 (mouse-click-edge prev buttons)
    in let s4 = poke-32 (m.um-state) 20 (peek-32 (m.um-state) 20 + 1)
    in let s5 = poke-32 (m.um-state) 0 um-ph-idle
    in let s6 = um-publish nx ny buttons
    in 1

  mouse-check : UsbMouse, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  mouse-check (m) (w) (h) =
    let code = xhci-wait-xfer (m.um-host) ((m.um-dev).ud-slot) (m.um-dci) 0
    in if code == 0 then 0
    else mouse-consume m w h

  mouse-pump-one : UsbMouse, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  mouse-pump-one (m) (w) (h) =
    if m.um-ok == False then 0
    else let ph = peek-32 (m.um-state) 0
    in if ph == um-ph-armed then mouse-check m w h
    else mouse-arm m

  mouse-pump-peers : List UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  mouse-pump-peers (ms) (i) (w) (h) (moved) =
    if i >= list-length ms then moved
    else let r = mouse-pump-one (list-at ms i) w h
    in mouse-pump-peers ms (i + 1) w h (if r /= 0 then 1 else moved)

  mouse-pump : UsbMouse, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  mouse-pump (m) (w) (h) =
    if m.um-ok == False then 0
    else let pm = mouse-pump-peers (m.um-peers) 0 w h 0
    in let r = mouse-pump-one m w h
    in if r /= 0 then 1 else pm
